sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control--gemini-4-argon--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Spherical Cahn-Hilliard phase-separation vs crystallographic anisotropy simulation" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> ;
    dct:description "Extend the Sumper (2002) and Lenoci-Camp (2008) hierarchical Cahn-Hilliard phase-separation model (already audited in morpholib as `sumper-diatom-frustule-phase-sep` for `thompson1917-ch09-14`) from flat diatom valves to spherical shells S^2 of radius R, and pit it against an anisotropic cellular-template / crystal-growth model. In the physical phase-separation model, organic polyamine droplets phase-separate on S^2 under isotropic interfacial tension gamma_0 and silica precipitates in the interstitial grooves; in the competing crystallographic/cytoskeletal control model, interfacial energy carries an m-fold director anisotropy gamma(theta) = gamma_0 (1 + epsilon_m cos(m theta)) representing either mineral crystal habit or cytoskeletal templating.\n\nSweep the dimensionless ratio R / l_c (shell radius to Cahn-Hilliard spinodal wavelength) and anisotropy strength epsilon_m across 200 simulation runs. Compare the simulated topological defect spectrum (number and spatial repulsion of 5- and 7-valent disclinations on S^2) and bond-orientational order parameter psi_6 against segmented 3D micro-CT/SEM reconstructions of spherical radiolarians (*Aulonia hexagonia*). Because Euler's theorem on S^2 forces topological charge sum(6 - z_i) = 12, pure isotropic surface tension predicts a specific scars-and-pleats disclination spacing as a function of pore count N_pores, whereas biological or crystallographic templating produces aligned defect lines." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Isotropic spherical Cahn-Hilliard phase separation (epsilon_m = 0) reproduces the measured radiolarian polygon-side variance mu_2, bond-orientational order psi_6, and 5-7 disclination scar length as a function of N_pores within 15% without invoking anisotropic templating." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Observed radiolarian or acantharian shells exhibit long-range azimuthal alignment of lattice axes or non-6-fold vertex symmetry that cannot be generated by isotropic Cahn-Hilliard or Plateau foam relaxation on S^2 (requiring epsilon_m > 0.1)." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement the spherical Cahn-Hilliard equation with variable m-fold interfacial anisotropy epsilon_m on S^2 using spherical harmonics or a geodesic icosahedral mesh. 2. Run a 200-point parameter sweep over pore count N_pores in [50, 2000] and anisotropy epsilon_m in [0, 0.3], extracting disclination pair-correlation functions g_55(r) and g_57(r) and bond-orientational order psi_6. 3. Measure the empirical disclination pair-correlation function and psi_6 on segmented spherical radiolarians and diatom valves. 4. Perform model selection (AIC / Bayes factor) between isotropic droplet phase-separation (epsilon_m = 0) and directed templating (epsilon_m > 0.05) on the empirical defect statistics." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: morpholib" ;
    skos:note "Tool: frontier_simulate" ;
    skos:note "Tool: geometry-statistics" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-15> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mikrotax.org/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1070026> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1021/la702278f> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1102289> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/gemini-4-argon.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How much of biomineral form is physical, and how much is cell-controlled?" .

  <https://www.mikrotax.org/> dct:title "Mikrotax radiolarian image archive" ;
    dct:description "Spherical polycystine radiolarian shells across pore-count scales for defect and psi_6 comparison" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1070026> dct:title "A Phase Separation Model for the Nanopatterning of Diatom Biosilica" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Sumper 2002, A Phase Separation Model for the Nanopatterning of Diatom Biosilica" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1021/la702278f> dct:title "Diatom Structures Templated by Phase-Separated Fluids" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Lenoci and Camp 2008, Diatom Structures Templated by Phase-Separated Fluids" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1102289> dct:title "Sea Urchin Spine Calcite Forms via a Transient Amorphous Calcium Carbonate Phase" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Politi et al. 2004, Sea urchin spine calcite forms via a transient amorphous calcium carbonate phase" .
}

